  "raw_output": "{\n  \"exercise_type\": \"CEFR English Placement Test\",\n  \"section_id\": 5,\n  \"section_title\": \"Reading Comprehension\",\n  \"cefr_range\": \"B2-C1\",\n  \"reading_text\": \"The concept of sustainability has become increasingly important in recent years, particularly in the context of environmental protection. However, despite growing awareness and efforts to reduce waste and emissions, many industries continue to prioritize short-term profits over long-term ecological health.\n\nSome argue that governments should impose stricter regulations on businesses to ensure they adopt more sustainable practices. Others claim that market forces alone can drive change, as consumers increasingly demand eco-friendly products. While both perspectives have merit, it is clear that a more holistic approach is needed.\n\nOne potential solution lies in the development of circular economy models, which aim to reduce waste and promote recycling. By designing products and services with sustainability at their core, businesses can not only minimize their environmental impact but also tap into growing consumer demand for eco-friendly options.\n\nHowever, critics argue that such initiatives often come at a significant cost, both financially and socially. They claim that the emphasis on sustainability has led to increased prices for consumers and created new social inequalities. It is essential to strike a balance between economic growth and ecological well-being.\n\nUltimately, achieving true sustainability will require a fundamental shift in our values and behaviors. By recognizing the interconnectedness of human and environmental systems, we can work towards a more equitable and regenerative future.\",\n  \"questions\": [\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 36,\n      \"question\": \"What is the main idea of the passage?\",\n      \"options\": [\"The importance of sustainability in business\", \"The need for governments to regulate industries\", \"The role of market forces in driving change\", \"The concept of circular economy models\"],\n      \"answer\": \"The importance of sustainability in business\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 37,\n      \"question\": \"According to the passage, what is a potential solution to achieving sustainability?\",\n      \"options\": [\"Implementing stricter regulations on businesses\", \"Focusing on short-term profits over long-term ecological health\", \"Developing circular economy models\", \"Relying solely on market forces\"],\n      \"answer\": \"Developing circular economy models\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 38,\n      \"question\": \"The passage suggests that the development of eco-friendly products is driven by which factor?\",\n      \"options\": [\"Government regulations\", \"Market demand from consumers\", \"Short-term profits\", \"Technological advancements\"],\n      \"answer\": \"Market demand from consumers\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 39,\n      \"question\": \"What is the author's attitude towards the emphasis on sustainability?\",\n      \"options\": [\"Critical and dismissive\", \"Neutral and objective\", \"Supportive and optimistic\", \"Cautious and nuanced\"],\n      \"answer\": \"Cautious and nuanced\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 40,\n      \"question\": \"The passage implies that achieving true sustainability will require a change in which aspect of human behavior?\",\n      \"options\": [\"Economic policies\", \"Social attitudes\", \"Environmental awareness\", \"Values and behaviors\"],\n      \"answer\": \"Values and behaviors\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
